course: world
lesson: 2
title: The seasons and the tilt of the axis
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Earth travels around the Sun once a year, and its axis is not upright but tilted, always leaning the same way in space.
explain: For part of the year the northern half leans towards the Sun: there the Sun climbs high, days are long, and it is summer.
explain: Half a year later the northern half leans away: the Sun stays low, days are short, and it is winter, while the southern half has summer.
explain: Low sunlight is weak because it spreads over more ground, just as a torch held at a slant makes a bigger, dimmer patch than a torch pointing straight down.
explain: The seasons do not come from the Earth being nearer to or farther from the Sun: the Earth is in fact closest to the Sun in early January, in the northern winter.
fact: The Earth goes around the Sun once in about 365 days, which is one year.
fact: The Earth's axis is tilted by about 23 and a half degrees.
fact: The axis keeps pointing the same way in space all year round.
fact: When the northern half of the Earth leans towards the Sun, it has summer.
fact: When the northern half has summer, the southern half has winter.
fact: In summer the Sun rises higher in the sky and days are longer than nights.
fact: In winter the Sun stays low in the sky and nights are longer than days.
fact: Sunlight that falls at a slant spreads over more ground, so it warms each piece of ground less.
fact: The seasons are not caused by the distance between the Earth and the Sun.
fact: The Earth is closest to the Sun in early January.
fact: Europe, Ukraine and Norway are in the northern half of the Earth; Australia and Cape Town in South Africa are in the southern half.
fact: When the Sun is low in the sky, shadows are long; when it is high, shadows are short.
fact: The equator is an imaginary line around the middle of the Earth, halfway between the North Pole and the South Pole.
fact: Near the equator the Sun is always high, so it is warm all year and the seasons are weak.
fact: Near the poles the Sun does not set for weeks in summer and does not rise for weeks in winter.
exercise: What causes the seasons? | The tilt of the Earth's axis as it goes around the Sun | The Earth coming closer to the Sun in summer | The Sun burning hotter in some months | The Moon blocking some sunlight in winter
exercise: How long does the Earth take to go around the Sun? | About one year | About one day and night | About one month | About ten years
exercise: Why is summer sunshine warmer than winter sunshine? | The Sun is higher, so its light falls more straight down | The Earth is much nearer to the Sun in summer than in winter | The Sun is bigger in summer | There is less air in summer
exercise: Why are days long in summer? | Our half of the Earth leans towards the Sun | The Earth spins more slowly in summer, so days stretch | The Sun goes around the Earth more slowly | The Moon reflects extra light in summer
exercise: When it is summer in Europe, what season is it in Australia? | Winter | Summer too | Spring time | It has no seasons at all
exercise: How does the Earth's axis point while it goes around the Sun? | Always the same way in space | Always straight at the Sun | Always straight away from the Sun | Up and down, changing every month
exercise: When is the Earth closest to the Sun? | In early January | In early July, in summer | In late March, in spring | In late September
exercise: Why does slanting light warm the ground less? | It is spread over a bigger area | It travels more slowly | It is a different colour of light | It is stopped by the Moon
exercise: Where are the seasons weakest? | Near the equator | Near the North Pole | Near the South Pole | In the middle of Europe
exercise: Why is it warm all year near the equator? | The Sun is high in the sky all year | The equator is closer to the Sun's fire | The sea there is heated from below | The Moon never shines there
exercise: What is special about summer near the poles? | The Sun does not set for weeks | The Sun is straight overhead at noon | The days are very short | It is as hot as at the equator
exercise: Why is it winter in the north in January even though the Earth is closest to the Sun then? | The north leans away from the Sun, so the light is low and the days short | The Sun is weaker in January | The north is covered by the Moon's shadow | The Earth's axis stands upright in January
exercise: How is a torch at a slant like the winter Sun? | Its light spreads over a bigger, dimmer patch | Its light becomes hotter and squeezes into a smaller spot | Its light changes colour to blue | Its light goes out sooner
exercise: In which season are nights longer than days? | Winter | High summer | Late spring | Every season the same
test: If the Earth's axis were not tilted at all, what would happen? | There would be almost no seasons | Every place would have a long summer | Every day would be twice as long | The Earth would stop going around the Sun
test: A girl in Kyiv and a boy in Cape Town talk in December. Which is true? | It is winter for her and summer for him | Both have winter | Both have summer | Both have autumn
test: Which is true about the seasons? | They come from the tilt, not from the distance to the Sun | They come from the distance to the Sun, not from the tilt | They come from the Moon going around the Earth | They come from the Sun moving up and down
test: In a model, a tilted ball goes around a lamp. When is its top half warmest? | When the top half leans towards the lamp | When the ball is farthest from the lamp | When the top half leans away from the lamp | When the ball spins fastest
test: At noon your shadow is very long. Which season is it most likely to be? | Winter | Summer | It cannot depend on the season | Any season near the equator
test: A town in Norway has the Sun in the sky at midnight. What season is it there? | Summer | Deep winter | Late autumn | It never happens there
test: Why does a slanting Sun make the ground cooler? | The same light is shared by more ground | The light has to cool down on the long way here | The Sun is farther away at a slant | The slanting light has no heat in it
test: Why is it that the north and the south never have summer at the same time? | When one half leans towards the Sun, the other leans away | The Sun can only shine on one pole a year | The Moon brings winter to one half | The south is always farther from the Sun
test: A plant grows best when the Sun is high and the days long. In which season will it grow fastest in the north? | Summer | Winter | Late autumn | The season makes no difference
test: why's it so cold in winter if the Sun's still out | The sunlight comes in low and slanted, so it warms less | The Sun is much farther away from us in winter than in summer | The Sun gives out less heat in winter | Winter clouds soak up all the Sun's heat
test: its summer here in july, so wots it in australia then | Winter, because the southern half leans away from the Sun | Summer too, it's summer everywhere in July | Spring, because it's always a season ahead there | Autumn, because Australia is closer to the Sun
test: Leo says we get summer cos we're closer to the sun. true? | No, it's from the tilt; we're closest to the Sun in January | Yes, summer comes when the Earth is nearest the Sun | Yes, the Earth swings close to the Sun every July | No, it's the Moon's distance that makes summer
test: a video said the Earth's axis flips over every winter to make the seasons. right? | No, the axis keeps pointing the same way all year | Yes, it flips every winter and turns back again in summer | Yes, but only by a small wobble each month | No, the axis flips only once every ten years
exam: Identify the principal reason for the occurrence of the seasons upon the Earth. | The inclination of the Earth's axis relative to its orbit | The variation in distance between the Earth and the Sun during the year | The alteration in the Sun's emission of heat | The passage of the Moon around the Earth
exam: It is given that the Northern Hemisphere is inclined towards the Sun. State the season then prevailing in the Southern Hemisphere. | Winter | Summer | Spring | No season whatsoever
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the date of the Earth's closest approach to the Sun. | It occurs in early January, during the northern winter | It occurs in early July, during the northern summer | It occurs at the spring equinox in March | It occurs at the autumn equinox in September
exam: Determine the reason for which oblique sunlight warms the ground to a lesser degree than sunlight arriving from overhead. | It is distributed over a greater area of ground | It travels a shorter distance from the Sun | It contains a greater proportion of cold rays | It is reflected entirely by the atmosphere
